# Target C — audited §168 explicit all-regime atomic Mantel gap

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module normalizes the stronger quantitative refinement developed in
`research/target_c_section164_explicit_atomic_gap_2026-09-12.md` and independently
checked in its hostile audit. It supersedes the weaker `10^-4` candidate.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\]

For every audited-working Type-II §142 train-measure subsequential limit, with
`x=lim T/D` and total atomic train-direction mass `sigma`, one has

\[
\boxed{x\sigma\ge 2s_2+0.0004.}
\tag{168.1}
\]

Thus the existential constant `eta_grid` from §164 may be taken to be `0.0004`.
No outer-detour hypothesis is used.

## 168.1 Dominant orbit concentration

Assume for contradiction

\[
A:=x\sigma\le \bar A:=2s_2+\eta,
\qquad \eta=0.0004.
\]

Group atomic masses by the exact projective orbit
`phi -> phi+pi/3 mod pi`. Let `M_r` be the absolute train mass in orbit `r` and `E_r`
its limiting strict-rhombus crossing mass. Audited §§142 and 164 give

\[
E_r\le {M_r^2\over4},
\qquad
\sum_rE_r=s_2^2,
\qquad
\sum_rM_r=A.
\]

If `M=max_r M_r`, then

\[
4s_2^2\le\sum_rM_r^2\le MA,
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{M\ge M_0:={4s_2^2\over\bar A}.}
\tag{168.2}
\]

The total atomic mass outside the dominant orbit is at most

\[
R_0:=\bar A-M_0,
\]

so its crossing mass is at most `R_0^2/4`. Therefore the dominant orbit carries at
least `s_2^2-R_0^2/4` crossing mass. A conservative Mantel deficit for that orbit is

\[
\boxed{
d_0:={\bar A^2\over4}-s_2^2+{R_0^2\over4}.
}
\tag{168.3}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\begin{aligned}
s_2&=0.9523128068639574\ldots,\\
\bar A&=1.9050256137279147\ldots,\\
M_0&=1.9042256977162868\ldots,\\
R_0&=0.0007999160116279\ldots,\\
d_0&=0.0003811250891520\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{168.4}
\]

The dominant orbit exists even with countably many atoms: a positive supremum of a
summable family is attained after restricting to the finite set above half that
supremum.

## 168.2 Quantitative finite Mantel stability

Freeze sufficiently small continuity windows around the at most three atoms of the
dominant orbit, then choose a sufficiently far finite source as in §164. The induced
triangle-free crossing graph has

\[
N=(M+o(1))D
\]

vertices and at least

\[
\left({M^2\over4}-d_0-o(1)\right)D^2
\]

edges.

Choose a maximum-degree vertex, put its neighborhood in `X`, and set `Y=V\setminus X`.
Triangle-freeness makes `X` independent. If `e_Y` is the number of edges inside `Y`,
maximum degree gives

\[
e+e_Y\le |X||Y|\le {N^2\over4}.
\]

Hence

\[
\left||X|-{N\over2}\right|\le(\sqrt{d_0}+o(1))D,
\tag{168.5}
\]

and the number of missing `X`--`Y` pairs is at most

\[
(2d_0+o(1))D^2.
\tag{168.6}
\]

For each of the three atom windows write normalized shore populations `x_i,y_i`.
Same-window pairs cannot cross once the windows have projective diameter below `pi/3`,
so

\[
\sum_i x_i y_i\le2d_0+o(1).
\]

Assign each atom window to its majority shore and discard the minority population.
Using

\[
\sum_i\min(x_i,y_i)
\le \sqrt{3\sum_i x_i y_i},
\]

the total discarded atom mass is at most `sqrt(6d_0)+o(1)`. Thus each shore retains
assigned atom mass at least

\[
\boxed{
S_0={M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0}-\sqrt{6d_0}
=0.8847704430706024\ldots .
}
\tag{168.7}
\]

There are at most three atom windows and both shores retain positive mass. Hence one
opposite-shore atom family has size at least `S_0D-o(D)` and another has size at least
`S_0D/2-o(D)`. Their limiting axes differ by exactly `pi/3` and their missing cross-pair
count is still at most `(2d_0+o(1))D^2`.

## 168.3 Pay separately for phase order and degree pruning

Put

\[
p_0:=\sqrt{2d_0}
=0.0276088786136620\ldots .
\tag{168.8}
\]

For projective lifts centered at axes differing by `pi/3`, every phase inversion is a
missing pair. The tie-safe Markov threshold argument from §163 therefore deletes at
most `p_0D+o(D)` trains from each family to obtain strict phase order.

This loss is distinct from the degree-pruning loss. After phase pruning, delete every
train missing more than `p_0D+o(D)` opposite trains. Since the total missing-pair count
is at most `(2d_0+o(1))D^2`, this removes at most another `p_0D+o(D)` trains per shore.

The retained families `A',B'` satisfy

\[
{|A'|\over D}\ge S_0-2p_0-o(1),
\]

\[
{|B'|\over D}\ge {S_0\over2}-2p_0-o(1),
\]

and every retained train misses at most `p_0D+o(D)` opposite trains.

Triple common neighbors are far from the threshold:

\[
{S_0\over2}-2p_0=0.3871674643\ldots
>3p_0=0.0828266358\ldots .
\]

Thus the actual-`K_{2,2}` consistency and triple-neighbor transitivity proof from §163
globalizes the crossing orders.

## 168.4 Opposite-corner diameter contradiction

Take the same opposite-corner two-leg path as §163. The two endpoint trains each lose
at most `p_0D+o(D)` neighbors. Therefore the selected degree sum is at least

\[
\left({3S_0\over2}-6p_0-o(1)\right)D.
\]

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
{3S_0\over2}-6p_0
=1.1615023929239314\ldots .
}
\tag{168.9}
\]

The phase-window widths tend to zero, so the common projection of every selected unit
train vector tends uniformly to `sqrt(3)/2`. Consequently the source diameter would
satisfy

\[
1\ge {\sqrt3\over2}(1.1615023929239314\ldots)-o(1)
=1.005890578828539\ldots-o(1),
\]

impossible.

This proves (168.1). The six `p_0` losses have explicit provenance: phase pruning on
each shore, degree pruning on each shore, and one corner-degree deficit on each shore.

## 168.5 Propagated all-regime floors

Audited §164 propagates the same atomic gain to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge2s_2+0.0004,
}
\tag{168.10}
\]

and, using `T-q=2f-k` and `q/D<=pi+o(1)`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {2f-k\over D}
\ge2s_2-{\pi\over2}+0.0004.
}
\tag{168.11}
\]

The §146 elimination gains exactly `(2/9)0.0004=1/11250`:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}
\left(h-{d_H\over9}-{10s_Z\over9}\right)
\ge {4\over9}(\pi+s_2)+{1\over11250}.
}
\tag{168.12}
\]

In particular,

\[
\liminf {h\over D}\ge1.819602426868333\ldots .
\]

## 168.6 Scope

- Atomic-mass semicontinuity is not used. Continuity windows are frozen inside one
  fixed subsequential limit before finite sources are chosen.
- Phase-order pruning and minimum-degree pruning are both paid explicitly.
- Unselected intervening rhombi only add positive projection, exactly as in §§76 and
  163.
- The constant `0.0004` is conservative; no larger value is certified here.
- No outer-detour hypothesis is used.
- Contact-isolated source points remain outside the train/rigidity kernel.

**Independent audit disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. C-II and
Level I remain open.
